exit code 1 together with the named probe above.

## 4. Limits and not-run items

- File modes on the `dc-warn` tree are not stable: it is a `fuse.rclone` mount, and mode bits have been
  observed to drift (this is PIT-274 from the captain lane). The guard file was normalized to `644`
  and `PATCH.diff` records `new file mode 100644`; a later mode drift on the worktree copy does not
  change file contents, and `FILES.sha256` covers contents only.
- Not run: `bun run verify`, `test:kotlin` (f64 Kotlin), `test:stage1:ts`, `test:rust`,
  `test:rust-f32`, `test:swift`, `test:swift-f32`, `test:dart`. The worktree carries in-flight
  changes from other agents (reflaxe Rust, Swift, and Dart compiler sources plus the
  `examples/*-f32.hxml` roots), and those bundles were not part of this change.
- `bunx tsc -p tsconfig.json` exits 2 with 9 pre-existing errors, all inside the generated
  `reference/ts/gen` tree (`TS18047`, `TS2322`). None is in a file this change touches; the guard
  file passes standalone strict `tsc` (exit 0) and ESLint (exit 0). See the
  `TSC_PROJECT_EXIT=2` entry in `evidence/lint-typecheck-docs.log`.
- The guard is not wired into a package script. `package.json` was left untouched because the task
  limits changes to the test file and the new guard tooling. Recommended follow-up: add
  `"check:precision-guard": "bun tools/precision-guard/check.ts"` and call it from `verify`.
- Guard scope: it masks comments, string literals, and `${...}` interpolation, and it treats `0x`,
  `0b`, and identifier digits as non-literals. It does not parse `EReg` literals specially (there is
  none in `samples/tests`), and it only reads `.hx` files.
- The fixed assertion no longer covers a binary64-only magnitude. That coverage lives in
  `samples/tests/MathNaNOpsTests.hx` through `MathNaNOps.constantOverflow()` with a comment that
  states the binary64 value and the binary32 flush.

